Fillets: a rounding whose walk crosses a slight concave crease rolling on
it is cut by the face beyond the crease instead, that face growing up to
it (mitcad#1349).

A fin's top runs as arcs and spline stretches; the arcs' edges are
chamfered (cones), the stretches' sides are a chamfer of the fin's root,
and a small transition piece of the arcs' chamfer (C, a ruled B-spline
face) lies between each cone and the root's chamfer (B). The crease between
B and C is concave by about 5 degrees, under the tenth of a radian
ChFi3d::IsTangentFaces takes for tangent. Rounding the top's edges with
0.2, the walk from the arc's torus (A) onto the stretch walked a piece over
C, rolled the ball on the crease and walked on over B (W): three faces
where the stored body has none. There the material the roundings take away
is kept outside C's material: A runs on past its end, cut by the stretch's
top (T) and by C up to the point Q where those cuts meet; W is cut by C from
P, where its contact on B crosses the crease, up to K, where its other
contact (on T) leaves C's material; and C grows over the gap up to T
between Q and K.

RoundingOverSlightCreases does so after a fillet's stripe is computed
(PerformSetOfSurf), for each piece rolling on an edge E that is no edge of
the spine, whose faces are tangent for OCCT but whose outward normals lie
more than a hundredth of a radian apart at E's middle, across a concave
crease: W is the walked piece beside it on B, ending on E, and A the first
piece on the other side whose faces are neither C nor T, analytic, its
contacts ending on its faces' edges with C and with T. Q is where the line
of T and C (their surfaces extended, ChFi3d_WideSurface) meets A's surface
past its end between its contacts, refined so that it lies on both faces'
surfaces; A's cuts by T and C are ChFi3d_KPartCutCurve's (T's surface, an
extrusion of a profile's stretch ending at the arc, run on past that end by
ChFi3d_RunOnExtrusionCurve), on A's own surface (one face with A). W's cut
is ChFi3d_NarrowCutPiece's (0531's), ending in a point at P, and the rest
of W is trimmed from K. The pieces between A and W are dropped, and the
stripe is split there into parts ending in points, stripes of their own
after the first (myNarrowParts, as 0531's), the last one taking over the
stripe's end at the spine's last vertex. C's growth is the curve of T and C
from Q to K (ChFi3d_FacesCurve). Where any of this fails, that transition
is left as the walk made it.

ChFi3d_KPartCutCurve gives a cut whose projection onto a surface fails (a
cut starting on the boundary of a walked blend's surface, at its end
section) the p-curve through its points' projections, the surface's bounds
widened a little (ChFi3d_PCurveByPoints).

On the fin's top (six transitions), on the design's own stored state before
the rounding (with 1351 and 1352): the result has the stored body's 134
faces (147 before); the grown pieces and the arcs' tori lie on the stored
body's faces within 1e-4 mm, the stretches' walked roundings within 0.0005
mm.

Test: geometry/tests/test_fillet_fin_top_creases.cpp,
test_transitions_cut_by_their_pieces, on the case `fin-top-chamfer-rounding`
of MITCAD_GEOMETRY_CASES (with 1351 and 1352).

Upstream: new.

